          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a code must never be sequenced first, how is that shown in the classification?",
          "answer": "The retrieved passages do not directly answer this. None of them describe a marker that says a code must never be sequenced first.\n\nThey do describe related signals in the ICD-10-CM conventions (from \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\"):\n\n- **Code first** and **use additional code** work as a pair and fix the order. The underlying condition is reported before its manifestation.\n- **Code also** means two codes are needed, without saying which comes first.\n- **Excludes1** means two conditions cannot both be present, so the codes are never reported together. **Excludes2** means both can be present and both may be reported. The passage was cut off before it fully covered Excludes1, so I can't give more detail on it.\n\nThe passages also note that for some pairs, such as acute or chronic respiratory failure (from \"Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44\"), the sequencing is decided by the circumstances of the encounter, not by a fixed rule.\n\nFor a definitive answer on \"never sequence first\" instructions, check the official ICD-10-CM Tabular List and its guidelines for the specific code. A trained medical coder can also confirm how it applies in a given case.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
